## Usage

### Basic
Clicking a thumbnail opens it full-window in [ImagePreview](/docs/dev/components/image-preview), where it zooms, pans, and swipes to page; `startIndex` picks the current preview image.
::::TuffDemoWrapper{demo="ImageGalleryImageGalleryDemo" code-lang="vue"}
---
code: |
  <script setup lang="ts">
  const items = [
    { id: 'one', url: '/images/one.png', name: 'Workspace overview' },
    { id: 'two', url: '/images/two.png', name: 'Release graph' },
  ]
  </script>

  <template>
    <TxImageGallery :items="items" :start-index="1" />
  </template>
---
::::

### Track Preview Opens

```vue
<script setup lang="ts">
function onOpen({ index, item }: { index: number, item: { id: string } }) {
  analytics.track('gallery_open', { index, imageId: item.id })
}
</script>

<template>
  <TxImageGallery :items="images" @open="onOpen" @close="onClose" />
</template>
```

### Controlled Starting Image
`startIndex` only selects the preview index; it never opens the lightbox.

```vue
<template>
  <TxImageGallery :items="screenshots" :start-index="selectedIndex" />
</template>
```

### Best Practices

- Use stable `id` values, not array indexes, for long-lived gallery data.
- Give informative images a `name` so button labels, alt text, and preview titles mean something.
- Keep the list modest: every thumbnail renders, with no virtualization.
- Normalize or proxy untrusted remote image URLs at your application boundary.
- Don't mutate `items` synchronously in `@open` in a way that invalidates the opened image.
- On non-English surfaces, override every text prop and both formatters, and pass the preview's `labelFormatter` through `previewProps`: the preview's buttons are icons, and these names are what screen readers announce; `failedText` is visible text.

## API Reference

### Props

| Prop | Type | Default | Description |
|------|------|---------|-------------|
| `items` | `ImageGalleryItem[]` | required | Images shown as thumbnails and in the fullscreen preview. |
| `startIndex` | `number` | `0` | Current preview index, clamped to the item range. |
| `previousLabel` | `string` | `'Previous image'` | Accessible name of the previous button. |
| `nextLabel` | `string` | `'Next image'` | Accessible name of the next button. |
| `previousText` / `nextText` | `string` | - | Deprecated and not shown: the preview's page buttons are icons, named by `previousLabel` / `nextLabel`. |
| `previewTitle` | `string` | `'Preview'` | The preview dialog's accessible name, followed by the current image's `name`. |
| `closeLabel` | `string` | `'Close'` | Accessible name of the preview's close button. |
| `zoomInLabel` | `string` | `'Zoom in'` | Accessible name of the preview's zoom-in button. |
| `zoomOutLabel` | `string` | `'Zoom out'` | Accessible name of the preview's zoom-out button. |
| `resetLabel` | `string` | `'Fit to screen'` | Accessible name of the preview's zoom readout, after the readout itself; clicking it shows the whole image again. |
| `failedText` | `string` | `'This image could not be loaded'` | Text in the preview's placeholder for an image that failed to load. |
| `itemLabelFormatter` | `(index: number) => string` | `` (i) => `Image ${i + 1}` `` | Builds a display name when `item.name` is absent. |
| `openLabelFormatter` | `(label: string) => string` | `` (label) => `Open ${label} preview` `` | Builds a thumbnail's `aria-label` from its display name. |
| `previewProps` | `ImageGalleryPreviewProps` | - | The preview's other options, such as `actions`, `indicator`, `bounce`, and the materials; the gallery sets the images, where they fly from, and the labels above. |

### Events

| Event | Payload | Description |
|------|---------|-------------|
| `open` | `{ index: number, item: ImageGalleryItem }` | Fires after a thumbnail opens the fullscreen preview. |
| `close` | `void` | Fires when the preview closes. |
| `action` | `ImagePreviewActionEvent` | Fires when an action is chosen in the preview, before it runs; after `preventDefault()` the host runs it. |

### ImageGalleryItem

| Field | Type | Description |
|------|------|-------------|
| `id` | `string` | Stable key for the thumbnail. |
| `url` | `string` | Image URL for the thumbnail and the preview. |
| `name` | `string` | Optional display name for labels, alt text, and the preview's name. |

## Overview

- Each thumbnail is a native `<button type="button">` whose `aria-label` comes from `openLabelFormatter`.
- Thumbnail and preview `alt` text is `item.name`; unnamed images deliberately get empty alt text.
- The preview traps Tab focus, closes on Escape, the close button, the backdrop, or dragging the image up or down, and returns focus to the thumbnail that opened it.
- Once open, the wheel, a trackpad, or two fingers zoom, and a drag pans a zoomed image; at fit, a sideways swipe or ← / → turns the page. At either end the page button is `aria-disabled` rather than disabled, so focus never drops to `body`.
- The top bar's image name and position count are an `aria-live` region, so screen readers announce each page.
- The preview grows out of the clicked thumbnail and shrinks back into it on closing. It opens on the whole image, never cropped, and never blows a small image up past its own pixels; at 640px wide or less, or 480px tall or less, the image fills the screen under floating controls.
- Until an image loads, its place is held at the shape of the last image looked at (4:3 for the first), and a shimmering placeholder appears only after 150 ms; with reduced motion, it does not shimmer.
- A preview image that fails to load shows a placeholder with `failedText`; the same URL is not retried, and a new `url` loads again.
- When `items` becomes empty, the preview closes.

## Technologies

- The preview is [TxImagePreview](/docs/dev/components/image-preview), teleported to `body`; its page has the gestures, keys, and placeholders in full. `TxAttachmentTray` opens the same preview, so the two behave alike. The on-demand style plugin loads the preview's styles along with it; when importing styles by hand, also import `style.css` from `image-preview`, `base-surface`, `flow-light`, and `glass-surface`.
